  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\nThe upper level ridge that has been over the CWA for the past few\ndays will begin to move eastward today, as two upper level\ndisturbances approach the CWA for Friday, one from the north and\none from the south. Today will be the last well above normal\ntemperature day for awhile, with many areas having high\ntemperatures in the upper 60s or lower 70s today. There will be a\nfew morning sprinkles over Southwest MT, but those should diminish\nearly this morning. Otherwise, rain showers start to approach the\nCWA from the southwest this evening and overnight. At the same\ntime, colder air will try to move southward from Canada overnight.\n\nThe colder air will be near the US/Canadian border by daybreak on\nFriday, as precipitation continues to overspread the western\nportions of the CWA. On Friday, expect the warmest temperatures in\nthe morning, with temperatures falling during the afternoon hours\nas colder air moves in. Snow levels will start out above 10,000\nfeet tonight, but then fall through out the day on Friday, likely\nfalling to near the valley/plains floor by Friday evening. Some\nshort periods of heavy precipitation is possible on Friday as the\nfront moves through. The front from the north will have some\nstrong north winds, and these strong winds could mix down as the\nprecipitation moves through. Thus a high wind watch has been\nissued for portions of North Central MT. At the same time, snow\nlevels will be falling, with the best accumulating snowfall above\n4500 feet. Thus some mountain ranges over Central/Southwest MT\nhave a winter weather advisory in place for Friday\nafternoon/evening for several inches of snowfall. At lower\nelevations, given the short duration of the precip and the time it\ntakes for the rain to change to snow, no advisory will be issued\nat this time.\n\nThis will be a very quick moving weather system, with both\ndisturbances exiting the region by early Saturday morning.\nGenerally dry conditions, but more fall like temperatures are\nexpected from Saturday through Tuesday. The next weather system\narrives next Wednesday, with a mix of rain/snow across the CWA.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\nChance for thunder...there is a small chance for a lightning\nstrike with the frontal passage on Friday. However, with a\nprobability mostly less than 10 percent, thunder was just confined\nto far Southwest MT for now.\n\nThe main concerns on Friday will be wind, rain changing to snow,\nand snow accumulations.\n\nStrong north winds are possible between Cut Bank and Havre on\nFriday, especially in the gap areas of the Sweet Grass Hills. with\na 70 percent chance for high wind gusts in the favored windy spots\nbetween Cut Bank and Havre, a high wind watch has been issued for\nportion of the day on Friday for this area.\n\nThe other main concern will be rain changing to snow, snowfall\nrates and snow accumulations on Friday. This system will be quick\nmoving, and the rain should change to snow within a few hours of\nfrontal passage. Additionally, some periods of heavy precipitation\nare possible, with snowfall rates exceeding 1 inch per hour at \ntimes. The heavy snowfall rates could exceed the warm pavement \nmelting, combined with the higher snowfall rates falling during \nthe Friday evening commute, some impacts are likely. Thus a winter\nweather advisory has been issued for the mountain areas over \nportions of Central and Southwest MT. It is possible additional \nwinter weather advisories might be needed for poor driving \nconditions at lower elevations on Friday evening, should the rain \nchange over to snow quicker, with the snow starting to affect the \nFriday evening commute at lower elevations. Brusda",
